export const SOURCES=[['Python CSV documentation','https://docs.python.org/3/library/csv.html'],['Investor.gov: stock split','https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/glossary/stock-split'],['Nasdaq Data Link: provider-specific adjustment documentation','https://data.nasdaq.com/databases/SEP/documentation']];
export const LESSONS={
provenance:{title:'A file needs a history.',objective:'CH3-01 · Identify provenance, units, date convention and adjustment basis before calculation.',body:`<p>A CSV is plain text: a header names columns, commas separate fields, and each subsequent line describes one observation. It is a storage format, not a guarantee of accurate data. A spreadsheet can open it, but may change dates automatically; preserve the original.</p><p>Our fictional asset LANTERN uses GBP per share. The dates are ISO YYYY-MM-DD and name observation dates; they are not timestamps. Prices are synthetic, with no exchange calendar claim. The file was authored for this course on 31 October 2026, version 1.0. No download of actual market history occurred.</p><div class="formula">date,raw,adjusted<br>2025-01-02,100,50<small>raw: price in the share units then in effect. adjusted: earlier prices divided by two for the fictional 7 January two-for-one split. No dividends or other actions.</small></div><p>Keep a data card: creator/source, version and retrieval date, instrument and currency, date/timezone convention, missing-value meaning, adjustment method, transformations and intended use. A real provider may define adjusted differently. Read its dictionary rather than borrowing our convention.</p>`,reflection:'Name the source, units and adjustment definition; describe one piece of evidence a real provider would need to supply.',criteria:'I identify synthetic provenance and split-only adjustment, and avoid calling these observed market results.'},
order:{title:'Time goes in one direction.',objective:'CH3-02 · Sort unique ISO dates and interpret returns over their actual observation intervals.',body:`<p>The first two rows in a file are not necessarily consecutive in time. Use dates to order observations from oldest to newest before calculating a return. ISO dates with fixed-width fields sort alphabetically in the same order as time. Other formats such as 02/03/2025 are ambiguous without a convention.</p><div class="formula">interval price return (%) = (later price ÷ earlier price − 1) × 100</div><p>Worked example: adjusted prices £50 on 2 January and £51 on 7 January give (51 / 50 − 1) × 100 = 2%. The result spans those dates. A gap does not reveal what happened inside it. Do not label it daily, average it across days or annualise it in this chapter.</p><p>Resolve duplicate dates before taking adjacent rows. The first retained observation has no preceding price, so its return is unavailable rather than zero. Consistent currency, share units and adjustment basis are required.</p>`,reflection:'Explain how an out-of-order row changes the meaning of a computed return, and how you would label a gap.',criteria:'I name both endpoint dates, distinguish an interval from a day, and leave the first return unavailable.'},
quality:{title:'A clean table needs decisions.',objective:'CH3-03 · Distinguish exact duplicates, conflicting observations and missing values; preserve an audit trail.',body:`<p>Inspect the seven-row file below. It contains a repeated identical observation, two conflicting prices for 3 January, and a row with blank prices on 6 January. A blank means unknown, not zero.</p><p><strong>Worked policy:</strong> retain one copy of an exact duplicate because date and both price fields agree. Quarantine all values for a conflicting date when no authoritative correction exists. Remove an observation with missing or invalid price. Record the removed rows and reasons; retain the untouched original. This conservative educational policy sacrifices information and is not a universal financial-data rule.</p><p>Never let a spreadsheet’s “keep last” silently decide truth. Forward filling assumes the last price persists; interpolation invents intermediate values. Such methods require a justified use case and labels. We perform neither. Exclusion does not prove that remaining data are accurate or representative.</p><p>Observed date gaps alone cannot establish which sessions should exist. A real audit also needs a verified market calendar, instrument availability, timezone and provider coverage. Here no expected-session count is supplied.</p>`,reflection:'Explain the three cleaning decisions and what information remains unresolved after removal.',criteria:'I distinguish exact duplication from conflicting evidence, record removals, and disclose the loss of information.'},
adjustment:{title:'A changed share is a changed unit.',objective:'CH3-04 · Explain split effects and calculate price returns from a consistent adjustment basis.',body:`<p>A two-for-one split replaces one share with two. Just before the fictional split, one share at £102 is worth £102. Just after, two shares at £51 are also worth £102. The unit price halves without a split-only wealth loss.</p><p>Our file rescales all pre-split prices by dividing by two. A raw £100 on 2 January becomes adjusted £50, while the later post-split £51 stays £51. The raw comparison looks like −49%; the split-consistent adjusted comparison is +2%.</p><div class="formula">raw: (51 / 100 − 1) × 100 = −49%<br>split-adjusted: (51 / 50 − 1) × 100 = +2%</div><p>Use the same basis at both endpoints. Never combine raw £100 with a later adjusted series and call the result comparable. This split-only file has no dividends. Its price return is not a general total-return measure; adding dividends to a provider series that already includes them could double count.</p>`,reflection:'Explain the raw-versus-adjusted difference using share counts, then distinguish a price return from dividend-inclusive total return.',criteria:'I explain unchanged split-only wealth and identify the dictionary’s exclusion of dividends.'}
};
